	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="text/markdown" rect="(20, 15, 520, 60)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**Chicago Crimes 2019 - what predicts an arrest?**  
MP1 workflow. Follow the numbered steps from left to right.</text>
		<text id="1" type="text/markdown" rect="(15, 250, 150, 110)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**1. Load**  
258,506 crime records with 22 attributes.</text>
		<text id="2" type="text/markdown" rect="(345, 140, 215, 150)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**2. Explore**  
Raw rows, missing-value counts per column, and the arrest rate for each crime type, to spot data problems.</text>
		<text id="3" type="text/markdown" rect="(180, 655, 330, 150)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**2b. Clean**  
Keep Primary Type, Domestic and Community Area as features and Arrest as the target; the other 18 columns are not used. Rows with a missing value are dropped.</text>
		<text id="4" type="text/markdown" rect="(480, 250, 210, 110)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**3. Dummy variables**  
One 0/1 column per crime type and per community area; Arrest and Domestic become 0/1.</text>
		<text id="5" type="text/markdown" rect="(730, 325, 190, 110)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**4. Split**  
80% training sample, 20% test sample, with a fixed random seed so results repeat.</text>
		<text id="6" type="text/markdown" rect="(1080, 15, 420, 85)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**5. Correlations**  
Pearson correlation of every feature with Arrest on the training sample. The 10 strongest are sent to *Top Correlations*.</text>
		<text id="7" type="text/markdown" rect="(1650, 400, 250, 170)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**6. Model**  
Linear regression with Arrest as the dependent variable, trained on the training sample and scored on the test sample. One dummy per category is dropped first as the baseline, so the coefficients are well defined.</text>
		<text id="8" type="text/markdown" rect="(1060, 900, 400, 85)" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="14">**7. Coefficients**  
Every coefficient of the trained model, then the 5 most positive and 5 most negative in *Top Coefficients*.</text>
		<arrow id="9" start="(75, 345)" end="(62, 392)" fill="#1F77B4" />
		<arrow id="10" start="(345, 215)" end="(300, 215)" fill="#1F77B4" />
		<arrow id="11" start="(255, 655)" end="(252, 628)" fill="#1F77B4" />
		<arrow id="12" start="(650, 360)" end="(700, 400)" fill="#1F77B4" />
		<arrow id="13" start="(850, 440)" end="(850, 528)" fill="#1F77B4" />
		<arrow id="14" start="(1165, 100)" end="(1162, 208)" fill="#39B54A" />
		<arrow id="15" start="(1650, 500)" end="(1592, 530)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<arrow id="16" start="(1465, 920)" end="(1500, 862)" fill="#C1272D" />
	</annotations>
